WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:07.120
The Predator gets a makeover — and gets outfoxed&nbsp;
by a dog. "Prey" is more than just "Predator 5."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.920
Here's how the prequel kept the franchise fresh.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.720
In "Predator 2," the Predator's ship&nbsp;
features artifacts and weapons spanning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.440
many times and cultures. As a result, each&nbsp;
new "Predator" film can really pit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.720
creature against any group of humans. Enter&nbsp;
"Prey," which follows Comanche warrior Naru&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.760
as she battles European soldiers and&nbsp;
the Predator in North America in 1719.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.000
In "Prey," our beloved Predator is&nbsp;
traveling light on a vacation to Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.880
While the humanoid alien is shown using its&nbsp;
invisibility cloak mode on multiple occasions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.080
the creature doesn't rely on any of its other&nbsp;
gadgetry as much as it has in previous films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.560
That isn't to say that all of the high-tech&nbsp;
alien weapons are absent, merely that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.240
era's Predator prefers to stab and impale its&nbsp;
prey rather than blast them to smithereens.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.320
"It fought and killed a bear with its hands."

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:03.040
Since the film does take place 300 years ago, it's&nbsp;
possible that this version of the Predator is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.760
quite fully evolved in the weapons department.&nbsp;
While the alien hunter is certainly far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.520
advanced than humanity in 1719, it doesn't&nbsp;
appear to have all the tricks and upgrades of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.480
"Alien vs. Predator." More importantly, this&nbsp;
makes it a much fairer contest between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.760
humans and the alien creature since it isn't&nbsp;
drawing upon an endless arsenal of options.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.920
However, it would be interesting to&nbsp;
witness how the creature fares without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.200
the use of its cloak technology at all, since&nbsp;
that still allows it to sneak up on humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.680
Animals, at least, can sniff the creature&nbsp;
out, even if they can't see the Predator.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.720
The success of John McTiernan's "Predator" film&nbsp;
can be attributed to its survival horror elements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.280
Dutch and his team are out in a jungle,&nbsp;
surrounded by trees. Constricting the space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.960
puts them at the mercy of the alien whose use&nbsp;
of camouflage and the environment makes members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.360
of the group sitting ducks. Similarly, other&nbsp;
"Predator" movies, especially the good ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:05.120
that try to honor the original, rely on this key&nbsp;
element, as the alien has a place to hide, sneak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.760
and strike from. To borrow a Terry Silver&nbsp;
quote from "The Karate Kid Part III,"

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.000
"If a man can't see, he can't fight."

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.200
"Prey" features some similar situations to&nbsp;
the first "Predator" where there are moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:23.600
of confinement. But it also poses a new question,&nbsp;
in asking how good the Predator is in open spaces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.280
The Great Plains boast vast amounts of&nbsp;
open land with only grass in the distance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.880
In theory, this would be the best place to lure&nbsp;
the alien creature to, since it removes the high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:41.360
ground aspect and lets any sudden movements be&nbsp;
more identifiable. However, "Prey" confirms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:48.080
the creature is as lethal in this setting as in&nbsp;
others. One especially memorable scene finds the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.200
beast chasing Naru and a fellow Comanche warrior&nbsp;
across an open field, a set piece reminiscent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.600
of the velociraptors stalking the hunters through&nbsp;
the long grass in "The Lost World: Jurassic Park."

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:04.960
The Predators hunt other life forms for both&nbsp;
sport and pride, claiming ritualistic trophies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.840
from their victories. They want to prove their&nbsp;
superiority, quite often in the most brutal way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.520
imaginable, while maintaining a code of honor&nbsp;
for their prey. This code is questionable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:21.040
though, since it isn't ever a fair fight if the&nbsp;
opponent fails to have intergalactic weapons in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.840
their possession. So it goes without saying&nbsp;
that their moral compass is a bit skewed.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.240
In other "Predator" films, the audience witnesses&nbsp;
the alien hunter go after humans as their main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:36.000
target, identifying them as worthy adversaries.&nbsp;
Ironically, humans aren't even considered apex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.360
predators in our own world, since we don't&nbsp;
eat everything we destroy. But in "Prey,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:47.200
the Predator goes after several creatures, not&nbsp;
just humans. It faces off in two big contests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.880
against a wolf and bear. The wolf shows&nbsp;
its nose is key in this encounter. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.520
it proves to be no match for the Predator's&nbsp;
sharp blades. On the other hand, the bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.720
strikes some real blows to the creature. If the&nbsp;
Predator's cloaking ability had been removed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:07.120
there's a good chance it might have struggled&nbsp;
to deal with the bear's sheer power.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:14.000
Next to its age-old nemesis the Xenomorph,&nbsp;
the Predator's design is one of cinema's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.800
most distinctive and iconic creatures. From its&nbsp;
trademark dreadlocks to its seriously gruesome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.600
face, the universe-traversing hunter would be&nbsp;
pretty easy to pick out in a lineup. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.840
in "Prey," the Predator looks a little different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.360
showcasing a leaner gladiator aesthetic.&nbsp;
While it's still easily identifiable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:39.040
it did receive a makeover with a few tweaks to its&nbsp;
physical appearance that fans noticed immediately.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.800
Dan Trachtenberg explained why he&nbsp;
felt it was necessary to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.840
the creature's appearance for&nbsp;
the film, telling Time Out,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.040
"I wanted to make sure the head&nbsp;
was more proportional to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.240
body [...] This Predator is much slimmer&nbsp;
and less armored than it's ever been.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.960
It's more 'creature.' It's&nbsp;
still hulking and ferocious."

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.280
At San Diego Comic-Con, Trachtenberg&nbsp;
also revealed that he and his team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.520
included goop around the Predator's face&nbsp;
to make it more disturbing to look at.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.360
In "Predator 2," the creature navigates&nbsp;
a concrete jungle with relative ease and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.400
fluidity. Despite the unique surroundings of&nbsp;
Los Angeles, it understands what it needs to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.440
to hunt and survive in this setting. The reason&nbsp;
for this is likely due to the events of "Prey,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:29.760
which serve as an important lesson for all the&nbsp;
other Predators of what not to do when on Earth.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.360
In "Prey," the Predator falls&nbsp;
into a quicksand-style mud trap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.400
which ultimately plays a major part in its&nbsp;
downfall. Like anyone else in such a pickle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.560
it struggles to get out of this situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.880
especially as it had been injured beforehand,&nbsp;
which made its predicament even worse. But it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.240
lead one to wonder if perhaps quicksand didn't&nbsp;
exist on any of the other planets the Predaor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.200
had visited before. It's also possible that&nbsp;
this Predator is still young and inexperienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.640
in its hunts. Maybe Earth is its first rite&nbsp;
of passage and it's trying to prove itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.840
quickly and recklessly. That would also explain&nbsp;
why the creature fights the bear in the river,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:13.520
when the water isn't conducive to its cloak mode's&nbsp;
effectiveness and helps to reveal its presence.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.600
Throughout all the "Predator" films, the&nbsp;
alien has taken on many formidable foes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.560
who have proven to be worthy adversaries&nbsp;
and even victors. From the terrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.680
Xenomorph to walking muscle mountain Dutch&nbsp;
Schaefer, played by Arnold Schwarzenegger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:35.200
the alien hunter has faced off against the&nbsp;
best of the best across the planet. In "Prey,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:40.560
the Predator gets too cocky for its own good,&nbsp;
failing to properly determine all of its threats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.600
Not only does the Predator dismiss Naru's&nbsp;
abilities, but it also overlooks her dog.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.560
While the Predator manages to put down a wolf, a&nbsp;
bear, and countless other human beings throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.800
the movie, it eventually falls to the outstanding&nbsp;
teamwork of Naru and her dog. One could even argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:02.080
that it's the dog that makes the difference&nbsp;
here, as Naru is left exposed in the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:07.040
fight against on two occasions. After previously&nbsp;
saving the Comanche warrior from the Predator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:11.280
the four-legged mutt appears at the end&nbsp;
of the film with an ax when Naru needs it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:16.240
most. In many ways, Naru's faithful&nbsp;
friend is the true hero of this story.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:22.160
Remaining quiet is often the key to&nbsp;
successfully capturing prey while hunting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.400
since it helps to maintain the element of&nbsp;
surprise, allowing the hunter to strike hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.920
and fast. In the previous "Predator" movies,&nbsp;
silence hasn't always been possible. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:37.440
everyone tries to keep their lips sealed and their&nbsp;
mobile phones on silent when in danger, groups of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:43.280
people tend to talk or move around, even when the&nbsp;
imminent threat of total annihilation looms near.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:44.800
"There's something else out there!"

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:52.000
On the other hand, "Prey" is a quiet film. Besides&nbsp;
the occasional bird chirp or rustle of the leaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:57.440
there's an intense stillness and quiet to the&nbsp;
Great Plains. In many ways, this setting is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:03.360
ideal for the Predator, as the silence ensures the&nbsp;
creature is able to hear the footsteps of its prey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:08.160
from a mile away. However, it's equally&nbsp;
effective for the intended victim, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:13.040
since any sudden movement will alert them&nbsp;
of a problem on the horizon. As an example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:16.400
Naru manages to find and&nbsp;
attack the unaware Predator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:22.080
simply because she's quieter than the alien. Talk&nbsp;
about using the hunter's tactics against them.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:27.840
There's an argument to be made that the "Predator"&nbsp;
franchise falls firmly under the umbrella of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.840
horror. After all, the whole concept of&nbsp;
an almighty alien hunter chasing after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:38.400
humans is frightening, plus the creature's&nbsp;
unmasked face is pure nightmare fuel. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:44.400
the franchise's previous films are jam-packed&nbsp;
with jump scares, with victims never knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:50.160
what's lurking around every corner. In "Prey,"&nbsp;
some of those scary elements remain, but there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:56.320
something different about this version. This time,&nbsp;
the Predator seems far more savage than before.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:02.960
This alien creature uses its raw power and&nbsp;
lightning speed to wicked effect. It moves with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:08.000
resolute purpose and uncanny brutality that&nbsp;
has been lacking in the franchise's recenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.160
outings. While brutality is a&nbsp;
key element of the slasher genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.960
the Predator isn't coming at people through&nbsp;
their nightmares or hiding behind doors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:21.440
walls. It doesn't hide in the shadows&nbsp;
like a boogeyman. Instead, it charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:27.280
into battle like a warrior that isn't afraid&nbsp;
of anything, which is somehow more horrifying.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:32.720
It's a foregone conclusion that the&nbsp;
better a warrior's class of weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:36.800
the better chance they have of success. In&nbsp;
the previous "Predator" films, the hunter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:41.680
shoulder cannons have made all the difference in&nbsp;
battle. In "Prey," the creature prefers to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:46.400
hand-to-hand combat to dispose of its opponents,&nbsp;
but it isn't averse to pulling out the big guns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:51.600
when absolutely necessary. But that doesn't&nbsp;
mean their superior weapons win out in the end.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.400
While literally stuck in sinking mud, the&nbsp;
Predator points its weapon at Naru and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.480
fires. However, it fails to realize&nbsp;
that it isn't wearing its helmet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:05.040
which features incredibly accurate targeting&nbsp;
capabilities. It also doesn't notice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:09.920
Naru has carefully set up the helmet to lock&nbsp;
the Predator in its crosshairs. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:15.280
the creature ends up on the receiving end of its&nbsp;
own attack. Again, it proves that technology can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:20.160
be useful in a fight, but a strategist will&nbsp;
always trump an aggressive brute in the end.

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:26.400
Consistent across the "Predator" franchise&nbsp;
is that the extraterrestrial beings believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:32.320
in a warrior's code. They don't hunt for a&nbsp;
tasty snack or out of self-defense. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:36.560
they take on opponents they believe will&nbsp;
provide a tough, daunting challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:41.920
or who would make excellent sparring partners,&nbsp;
like in the Yakuza swordfight in "Predators."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:47.840
For these alien hunters, it's about testing&nbsp;
their superior skills and advanced smarts as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:52.800
they attempt to prove they are worthy enough to&nbsp;
be a part of their clan. Throughout the "Predator"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:58.720
films, this code seems to remain intact.&nbsp;
However, "Prey" explores what happens if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.840
alien isn't actually all that good at identifying&nbsp;
threats or challengers, for whatever reason.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:05.840
"This is as far as you go."

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:11.360
Undoubtedly, the Predator sees the Comanche&nbsp;
warriors as worthy enough to challenge and hunt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:15.760
It stalks and battles them, coming out&nbsp;
on top on several occasions. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:20.720
the creature fails to see Naru as a threat from&nbsp;
the get-go. She demonstrates a warrior spirit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:25.440
and natural fighting ability, even if some&nbsp;
of her own people doubt her skills. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:31.120
the Predator basically scoffs at the thought of&nbsp;
taking her on. But it's Naru's resilience and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:36.320
ingenuity that bring down the alien hunter&nbsp;
in combat. Imagine how different "Prey"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:42.453
would have been had the Predator never&nbsp;
underestimated Naru in the first place.

